       ppaappeerrssiizzee - specify preferred paper size

SSYYNNOOPPSSYYSS
       //eettcc//ppaappeerrssiizzee

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ppaappeerrssiizzee  file is used to specify the preferred paper size to use
       by available commands and programs generating documents.

       The format of this file is extremely simple:  whitespace  and  anything
       starting  with  `#'  is ignored, and the name of the paper is the first
       string found; the case in the name of the paper does  not  import  (see
       CCAAVVEEAATTSS section however).

PPAAPPEERR NNAAMMEESS
       The  following  names  are commonly understood by programs: aa33, aa44, aa55,
       bb55, lleetttteerr, lleeggaall, eexxeeccuuttiivvee, nnoottee and 1111xx1177.

       Additional paper names that one may encounter are: aa00, aa11, aa22, aa66,  aa77,
       aa88,  aa99, aa1100, bb00, bb11, bb22, bb33, bb44, ttaabbllooiidd, ssttaatteemmeenntt, nnoottee, hhaallfflleetttteerr,
       hhaallffeexxeeccuuttiivvee, ffoolliioo,  qquuaarrttoo,  lleeddggeerr,  aarrcchhAA,  aarrcchhBB,  aarrcchhCC,  aarrcchhDD,
       aarrcchhEE, ffllssaa, ffllssee, ccsshheeeett, ddsshheeeett, eesshheeeett and 1100xx1144.

       The  value  of the ppaappeerrssiizzee file can be overridden by looking in order
       at the PPAAPPEERRSSIIZZEE environment variable, then at the contents of the file
       specified by the PPAAPPEERRCCOONNFF environment variable.  If the ppaappeerrssiizzee file
       does not exist, programs using the paper library default to using  lleett‐‐
       tteerr as a fall-back value

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
       This  manual  page  documents  the format of the ppaappeerrssiizzee file that is
       read by the lliibbppaappeerr library.  Some programs that read this file do not
       yet  use  the library and may have trouble ignoring whitespace and com‐
       ments in the file; they may also require that the  paper  names  use  a
       specific capitalization.

